  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Fetch Aborts**\n\nI\'m now investigating the server-side abort errors, specifically the fetches initiated in `server.ts`. The OpenSky endpoint, with its substantial payload of flight data, is the immediate focus, as it is a prime candidate for timeouts or interruptions. I\'ll need to figure out why these requests are being aborted, which might involve network conditions or request size limitations.\n\n\n**Adjusting Request Timeouts**\n\nI\'ve realized the 6-second timeout is insufficient for some API calls, particularly OpenSky, given its size and potential for network latency. This is a problem in Cloud Run environments where cold starts or network issues could be more likely, and 6s is too short to work reliably, or at all. I will increase the timeout and implement a caching mechanism in `server.ts` to handle failures more gracefully, by checking if cached data exists, and returning the most recently validated, cached dataset if the network call fails.\n\n\n**Improving Cache Handling**\n\nI\'m now concentrating on refining the cache behavior. Previously, expired cache entries were discarded, even with upstream network glitches, leading to `502` errors. I\'m increasing timeouts to 15 seconds to prevent premature request terminations. Now, if upstream calls fail, I\'ll return a stale cached version with a \"CACHED (UPSTREAM SLOW)\" status, improving reliability. I will also incorporate rate limiting for OpenSky and implement a fallback strategy to other APIs when it is unreachable.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 3
      nanos: 456023161
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
